# This machine's settings for metacomputer. install.sh copies this
# file to config/local.env (never committed) and fills in what it asked. Any
# value in config/defaults.env can be overridden here; the common ones are
# listed. It is read after config/defaults.env, as plain assignments only; anything
# else refuses: every line is blank, a # comment, or one NAME=value, NAME="value" or
# NAME='value' (optionally after export, with a # comment after). The handoff check of a
# program run by itself reads it without running it, and refuses the whole file for any
# other line (a command, an if, a source line, a line continued with \ or chained with
# &&, an unquoted value with a space or a backslash) and for a line that sets HOME or
# LAB_ROOT.

# --- The VM -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lume VM names. Change them to keep several virtual computers side by side.
#VM_NAME=metacomputer
#BASELINE_NAME=metacomputer-baseline
#VM_HOSTNAME=metacomputer
#VM_CPU=4
#VM_MEMORY=8GB

# Browsers baked in at build time ("firefox" or "firefox chrome"). Chrome means
# accepting Google's terms of service.
#BROWSERS="firefox chrome"
# More Ubuntu packages built into the VM (next build), without "recommended" extras.
#EXTRA_PACKAGES="inkscape audacity"
# A sound card inside the VM that plays into nothing (next build); recordings capture it.
#VIRTUAL_AUDIO=on

# Which Lume runs the VM (install.sh --lume=mcomp-vm|standard sets this):
#LUME="$HOME/.local/bin/mcomp-vm"   # or "$HOME/.local/bin/lume" for standard Lume

# Host sharing. With mcomp-vm each is left out of the VM unless "on":
#SHARE_CLIPBOARD=off   # on = the VM shares your Mac's clipboard (both ways)
#SHARE_AUDIO=off       # on = the VM gets your Mac's speakers and microphone
#SHARE_ROSETTA=off     # on = x86-64 Linux programs run through Rosetta

# --- Host side ----------------------------------------------------------------
# SSH keys for the VM (generated by the build, kept outside the repository).
#SSH_DIR="$HOME/.ssh/metacomputer"
# Loopback ports on this Mac for the VM's SSH and live view.
#SSH_PORT=2277
#VNC_PORT=5977

# Where the live-view (VNC) password is kept:
#   keychain  the login Keychain, item metacomputer-vnc, account VM_NAME; a new one
#             each build. Read it with: security find-generic-password -s metacomputer-vnc -a VM_NAME -w
#             [what install.sh writes below for a new setup; a password already in
#             $SSH_DIR/vnc-pass stays in use, and a locked Keychain falls back to it]
#   file      a random password in $SSH_DIR/vnc-pass (0600)  [the default without a setting]
#   command   your own secret store (a password manager CLI):
#             VNC_PASSWORD_GET prints the password, VNC_PASSWORD_SET stores the
#             password it reads on stdin
#VNC_PASSWORD_STORE=keychain
#VNC_PASSWORD_GET="my-secret-tool get metacomputer/vnc"
#VNC_PASSWORD_SET="my-secret-tool set metacomputer/vnc"
# `metacomputer view --open` puts the password on your clipboard and clears it after this many
# seconds, if the clipboard still holds it (0: never cleared)
#VIEW_CLIPBOARD_S=45
# Screen Sharing's remembered password (the login keychain item Screen Sharing reads for the live
# view): ask (the first time, at a terminal) | on (save without asking) | off (no writes at all)
#VIEW_REMEMBER=ask
# Allow metacomputer to show dialogs on this Mac that wait for you (on: a handoff shows one with
# an "Open live view" button, and `secrets ask` one with a hidden field for the code). Off by
# default: nothing pops up on your screen by itself.
#INTERACTIVE_PROMPTS=off

# --- A second VM beside the first ---------------------------------------------------
# Simplest: `metacomputer vm create second` writes config/vms/second.env with all of
# this (and its own vault folder) and builds it; then `metacomputer --vm second ...`.
# By hand, in a file used with MCOMP_CONFIG=that-file: its own names, keys, ports and
# folders, so the two never share a gateway, a lock or logs:
#VM_NAME=second
#BASELINE_NAME=second-baseline
#SSH_DIR="$HOME/.ssh/metacomputer-second"
#SSH_PORT=2299
#VNC_PORT=5999
#RUN_DIR="$HOME/.metacomputer-second/run"
#LOG_DIR="$HOME/.metacomputer-second/logs"

# --- Storage (docs/storage.md) ---------------------------------------------------
# metacomputer volume create work --size 20G; metacomputer refdisk create docs --from ~/some/folder
#STORAGE_DIR="/Volumes/External/metacomputer"   # where new ones go (default ~/.local/share/metacomputer)
#VOLUMES="work"                    # in the VM at ~/data/work, read-write, kept across reset
#VOLUMES="work:/volume"            # at /volume, like Cua Spaces' volume (also /mnt, /data, /srv, /workspace)
#REFDISKS="docs:/mnt/docs"         # read-only; any number of VMs
# metacomputer volume create team --s3 --bucket my-bucket --endpoint https://s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com
# metacomputer volume keys team     (in your own terminal: the keys go into the macOS Keychain)
#VOLUMES="team:/mnt/team:rw"       # a local copy of the bucket, synced at start, on the interval and at stop
#S3_SYNC_INTERVAL=60

# --- Desktop recordings (metacomputer record) -----------------------------------
#RECORD_FPS=30
#RECORD_QUALITY=high        # sharp enough to edit into videos; several times larger
#RECORD_TO=guest            # record inside the VM and copy at stop; until then anything in the VM can
                            # delete it (the default, host, streams to this Mac as it records)
#RECORD_DIR="/Volumes/External/recordings"   # any folder; only its last part is created
#RECORD_AUDIO=off           # leave the VM's sound out (auto includes it when VIRTUAL_AUDIO=on)
#TASK_SYNC_INTERVAL=0       # copy a task's recording only at task finish (default: finished turns every 15 s)

# --- What Claude does by default (metacomputer browser default|mode|pacing) ---------
#BROWSER=firefox     # firefox | chrome
#MODE=human          # human | page | hybrid
#BROWSER_PACING=fast # human (default): the hub types and clicks in browser windows at a person's pace; fast: no pauses

# --- The hub (one connection per VM, shared by every client; architecture.md "The hub") ----
#MCP_HUB=off                # on (default): Claude Code's bridge, `call` and scripts share one hub per VM;
                            # off: each connects on its own (the same guards, run in each client)
#HUB_IDLE=10m               # the hub exits after this long with no client and no driver recording
#COORD_GUARD=off            # on (default): pixel input refused when the windows changed since its screenshot
#HIDDEN_TEXT=off            # on (default): hidden and instruction-like page text marked in results for Claude

# --- Lessons from your own sessions (metacomputer learn; docs/learn.md) ---------------
#LESSONS=off                # on (default): approved lessons go in every new session's MCP instructions
#LESSONS_DIR="$HOME/.local/share/metacomputer/lessons"   # the private store (0700 folder, 0600 files)

# --- One-time codes (SECURITY.md "One-time codes and sign-in links") ------------------
#CODES_VIA=claude           # vault (default): one-time codes go through the vault; claude: Claude may read and
                            # type them itself (they land in the transcript; no site binding). Guidance only

# --- Shell channel (metacomputer sh; docs/shell.md) ---------------------------------
#VM_SHELL=off               # auto (default): on, but off while DRIVER_MODE=bounded; on: always
#SHELL_LOG=off              # auto (default): logged when ACTION_LOG=on; off: never logged

# --- Optional hardening (SECURITY.md) -------------------------------------------
# All off by default, which is how Cua Driver behaves on its own.
#ACTION_LOG=on              # signed, hash-chained log of every tool call (metacomputer actions)
#ACTION_LOG_TEXT=redact     # keep = record typed text, values and scripts in full
#ACTION_LOG_KEY="$HOME/.ssh/my-agent-key.pub"   # sign with a key held in ssh-agent instead of a file
#SSH_AUTH=cert              # a fresh key and a short-lived SSH certificate per connection
#SSH_CERT_TTL=5m
#SSH_CA_KEY="$HOME/.ssh/my-agent-ca.pub"        # a CA key held in ssh-agent instead of $SSH_DIR/ca
#DRIVER_MODE=bounded        # Cua Driver does only what a manifest allows
#DRIVER_PROFILE=desktop     # readonly | desktop | browser (config/driver-profiles/)
#DRIVER_ORIGINS="https://example.com https://app.example.com"   # browser profile: the sites it may open
#DRIVER_MANIFEST="$HOME/my-manifest.yaml"       # your own Cua capability manifest instead of a profile
#DRIVER_EXPIRES=8h          # a driver session's lifetime (at most 24h)
#DRIVER_IDLE=30m            # it ends after this long without a call

# A Python that has Oracle's SDK (pip install oci), for a computer Oracle Cloud makes (docs/remote.md).
# Only lib/remote.py runs with it; the credentials come from the environment of the command, never this file.
# OCI_PYTHON="$HOME/.local/share/metacomputer/oci/bin/python"
